WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.920
Can a still photo tell you how long something takes?

2
00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.240
A photo freezes one moment, and nothing in it moves.

3
00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:12.960
So where would the time be hiding?

4
00:00:14.067 --> 00:00:19.247
The first film followed one model cell through the cycle, step by step, in order.

5
00:00:20.507 --> 00:00:22.427
A stained root tip cannot show you that.

6
00:00:23.587 --> 00:00:24.867
It gives you only a snapshot.

7
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.440
Picture a huge school with no shared bell.

8
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.580
Each student keeps a different timetable.

9
00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:34.840
Snap a photo at a random moment.

10
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.360
Most students are in class.

11
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.100
Only a few are in the hallway.

12
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:42.500
Why?

13
00:00:42.933 --> 00:00:46.153
Class lasts much longer than the walk between classes.

14
00:00:47.353 --> 00:00:51.373
So at any random moment, a student is more likely to be in class.

15
00:00:52.567 --> 00:01:00.247
Try it yourself. Stage A lasts twice as long as Stage B. In a random snapshot, which

16
00:01:00.247 --> 00:01:01.527
stage will you see more of?

17
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:15.300
Stage A. You should see about twice as many things in Stage A. A stage that lasts

18
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.720
longer tends to hold more of them in a snapshot.

19
00:01:18.883 --> 00:01:20.743
Now swap the students for cells.

20
00:01:22.063 --> 00:01:25.163
Set an onion on a glass of water, and roots grow down.

21
00:01:26.443 --> 00:01:28.783
Near each root tip, cells divide often.

22
00:01:29.867 --> 00:01:34.047
A thin root tip is dyed, or stained, so the chromosomes show up.

23
00:01:35.247 --> 00:01:39.027
Under a microscope, each cell is caught at one moment of its own cycle.

24
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:44.500
Each cell is sorted into a stage by what its chromosomes look like.

25
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.940
Thick, coiled chromosomes, not yet in a row, mean prophase.

26
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.400
A row across the middle means metaphase.

27
00:01:53.383 --> 00:01:56.483
Let's work one stage together, using real counts.

28
00:01:57.783 --> 00:02:03.163
In one set of 36 photographed onion cells, 10 were in prophase, the coiled stage.

29
00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:06.670
Turn that count into a share of all the cells.

30
00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:09.230
Follow the steps on the screen.

31
00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.210
Prophase holds about 28 percent of the cells.

32
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:18.550
The activity says to assume one cycle takes 24 hours.

33
00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.970
Take each stage's share of the cells as its share of the time.

34
00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.390
Then prophase lasts about 7 hours.

35
00:02:27.483 --> 00:02:29.083
Treat this as a rough guide.

36
00:02:30.363 --> 00:02:34.003
There are only 36 photos, and nobody says how they were picked.

37
00:02:35.223 --> 00:02:38.043
And the 24 hours is assumed, not measured.

38
00:02:39.117 --> 00:02:43.977
Still, it works best with many fairly picked cells, dividing out of step in a

39
00:02:43.977 --> 00:02:45.077
steadily growing root.

40
00:02:46.417 --> 00:02:49.697
Then a longer stage tends to hold more cells in a snapshot.

41
00:02:50.997 --> 00:02:54.697
So a stage's share of the cells is only a rough guide to its time.

42
00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:57.090
That was one stage.

43
00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:01.750
Four more are waiting, along with the biggest question in the lesson.

44
00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.330
Now count the rest yourself.
